# XCU man
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# man with standard output redirected to a file; the content and layout of
# reference text are implementation-defined, so the tests look only at
# success, the presence of output and, for -k, the shape of the matching
# lines. PAGER is set to cat so that nothing can block.

assertion  XCU.man.001
anchor     utilities/man.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/man.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/man.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       For the name of a standard utility, man exits 0 and writes a non-
           empty description to standard output, and with two such names it
           still succeeds and writes at least as much.
test       t/001-standard-utility.sh

assertion  XCU.man.002
anchor     utilities/man.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/man.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       With -k each output line matches at least one of the keyword
           operands, ignoring case (tested when the summary database has any
           line for the keywords sort and copy; the wording of purpose
           entries is unspecified).
test       t/002-keyword-search.sh

assertion  XCU.man.003
anchor     utilities/man.html#OPTIONS
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       A keyword given in upper case finds lines for the same purpose
           entries as the lower case keyword.
test       t/003-keyword-case.sh

assertion  XCU.man.004
anchor     utilities/man.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
anchor     utilities/man.html#DESCRIPTION
class      untestable
since      1992
says       When standard output is a terminal the text is piped through the
           PAGER command, or a default paginator when PAGER is null or
           unset.
reason     It needs a terminal on standard output, which a script run by the
           driver does not have; the wording of the reference text is also
           implementation-defined.
